1
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:42,680
It'll work out somehow.

2
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:47,940
You get it, right? Got it, got it.

3
00:00:47,940 --> 00:00:51,860
If you get it, shut up and follow me.

4
00:01:05,460 --> 00:01:07,660
The masterpiece "Momoderella."

5
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:13,400
Yo.

6
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:16,100
Senpai.

7
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:19,320
What are you all doing gathered
together first thing in the morning?

8
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:23,020
You do the nursing home comfort visits every year,
don't you?

9
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:26,840
We were deciding what play to put on there.

10
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:31,580
A comfort play?
I had no idea you did that every year.

11
00:01:31,980 --> 00:01:37,460
Inviting someone like you, who doesn't know the
first thing about volunteering, won't get you any help.

12
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:40,200
You're a man who thinks only about money,
money, aren't you?

13
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:41,840
Hah, of course.

14
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:46,300
A pro-level multi-talent like me,
who can sing, dance, and make people laugh,

15
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:48,200
would never do it for free.

16
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:51,160
What's this? A picture book?

17
00:01:52,180 --> 00:01:54,120
Momotaro and Cinderella?

18
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:58,760
You're not seriously planning to put on a kiddie play like this at a nursing home,
are you?

19
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:02,000
You're not doing it for free, right?
Don't butt in.

20
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,400
As an entertainer,
I thought I'd say a word or two.

21
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:10,240
Along with us, kids from the nearby
kindergarten will be taking part in the visit.

22
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:14,360
We're putting on a play that's easy to
understand for both the elderly and the kids.

23
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:17,800
Still, Momotaro and Cinderella? Come on.

24
00:02:19,780 --> 00:02:25,320
Fairy tales are good for education, and last
year's Kaguya-hime got a huge ovation at the end.

25
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:31,060
What's good about that for education? I
think it'd only be a bad influence on the kids.

26
00:02:31,060 --> 00:02:34,020
This Momotaro, in particular, is fishy.

27
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,360
When it comes to Momotaro,
it's a classic of Japanese folk tales.

28
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:42,940
A human being born from a peach
is already far too unrealistic.

29
00:02:42,940 --> 00:02:47,460
Even if a peach pit mutated,
it could never turn into a human.

30
00:02:49,320 --> 00:03:19,300
If it turned into a human,
the greengrocer would be unbearably noisy.

31
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,280
So, of course, the Momotaro inside...

32
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:28,400
When it gets cut,
it has to stick to the side or something.

33
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:32,540
Normally,
people don't think that far, do they?

34
00:03:32,540 --> 00:03:33,440
Honestly.

35
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:35,100
That's not true at all.

36
00:03:35,100 --> 00:03:37,780
This whole story is full of contradictions,
you know.

37
00:03:37,780 --> 00:03:42,720
First, a huge peach comes drifting down the river,
bobbing along, bobbing along.

38
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:44,060
Yes, that's right.

39
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:45,940
Don't you think that's strange?

40
00:03:45,940 --> 00:03:47,060
Not really.

41
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:49,400
There's a Momotaro inside the peach,
you know!

42
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:51,120
It's unnatural for it to float.

43
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:56,020
The natural thing would be for it to roll along the riverbed,
rumbling.

44
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:59,700
Maybe there was air inside the peach.

45
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:02,620
Then, giving you a hundred steps of leeway,
let's say it was floating.

46
00:04:02,620 --> 00:04:04,960
If the baby inside is this big,

47
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:07,620
the peach would have to be this big,
wouldn't it?

48
00:04:07,620 --> 00:04:09,760
To be realistic, it's creepy.

49
00:04:10,360 --> 00:04:13,080
If a giant peach like this
came floating down the river,

50
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:15,380
Nyohee, peach, peach, peach!

51
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:21,180
There's no way she'd have
wanted to eat it at home, though.

52
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:22,860
Well, that's probably true.

53
00:04:22,860 --> 00:04:26,840
Even if I give you a thousand steps, let's say
the old woman took it home meaning to eat it.

54
00:04:26,840 --> 00:04:29,620
But you eat fruit after a meal, right?

55
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:31,820
Normally, yeah.

56
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:36,000
She could also forget
about it and let it rot.

57
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,920
It's an old folk tale,
so there are story conveniences too.

58
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:42,420
So she doesn't need to be born from
a peach just for the sake of it.

59
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:44,900
Then it's fine to say they
found him at a shrine, right?

60
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:47,520
But then he wouldn't be Momotaro, would he?

61
00:04:47,520 --> 00:04:52,600
You could make up any excuse, like they named him
Momotaro because they found him in peach season.

62
00:04:53,780 --> 00:04:55,360
And then there's this demon-slaying.

63
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:57,700
They suddenly go raid Oni Island.

64
00:04:57,700 --> 00:05:00,300
They beat up the demons,
steal their treasure, and head home.

65
00:05:00,300 --> 00:05:02,860
What they're doing is the
same as a gang of robbers.

66
00:05:03,380 --> 00:05:09,060
Even if the demons are bad guys, if Momotaro and his
crew pulled this today, they'd be arrested for sure.

67
00:05:11,660 --> 00:05:14,580
Even more far-fetched are the retainers.

68
00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:16,000
Retainers?

69
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:17,260
What are the retainers?

70
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:19,200
Let's see, a monkey,

71
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:20,040
a dog,

72
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:21,260
and a pheasant, right?

73
00:05:21,260 --> 00:05:24,600
Do you really think pet-like animals
like those would be of any use?

74
00:05:25,160 --> 00:05:27,520
The monkey scratches, the dog bites,

75
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:30,440
and the pheasant pecks the demon's eyes out,
and that's how they defeated it?

76
00:05:30,440 --> 00:05:34,360
The enemy is a demon as
terrifying as Chief Ohara!

77
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:36,460
Are you messing with me or what?

78
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:38,840
One swing of a kanabō and you're done for.

79
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:40,700
Th-that's true, isn't it?

80
00:05:41,160 --> 00:05:42,700
If I were Momotaro,

81
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:46,000
I'd never take such weak retainers with me.

82
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:47,260
If I were taking any,

83
00:05:47,260 --> 00:05:48,960
first, a lion.

84
00:05:48,960 --> 00:05:50,560
Next, a tiger.

85
00:05:50,560 --> 00:05:52,600
And a grizzly bear.

86
00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:55,200
With these, even Chief Ohara

87
00:05:58,020 --> 00:05:59,620
Yes, that's the one, that's the one!

88
00:06:11,960 --> 00:06:12,960
Senpai

89
00:06:12,960 --> 00:06:16,660
Yo!

90
00:06:16,660 --> 00:06:24,140
Don't go using me as
an image without asking!

91
00:06:24,140 --> 00:06:26,500
Damn it, what are you doing here?

92
00:06:27,440 --> 00:06:29,860
Because if the Chief barges in again,
it'll be a problem.

93
00:06:29,860 --> 00:06:32,440
The retainers could be a monkey,
a dog, or a pheasant.

94
00:06:32,440 --> 00:06:36,120
But do you really think a dog would
become a retainer for a millet dumpling?

95
00:06:36,120 --> 00:06:39,060
Komachi, if you're the monkey,
would you become a retainer?

96
00:06:39,060 --> 00:06:41,560
I won't do it for millet dumplings!

97
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:42,480
Hey.

98
00:06:42,940 --> 00:06:45,160
Right? They're going out to slay demons,
you know.

99
00:06:45,160 --> 00:06:46,740
when they might not even survive it.

100
00:06:46,740 --> 00:06:49,280
Nobody's going to become a
retainer for just millet dumplings.

101
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:51,480
Cash is the obvious choice here, right?

102
00:06:51,480 --> 00:06:52,920
W-well, yeah.

103
00:06:53,520 --> 00:06:55,560
Or diamonds, or designer goods.

104
00:06:56,500 --> 00:06:57,840
A house within two hours of the city.

105
00:06:57,840 --> 00:07:00,340
If it were me,
I'd want a brand-new motorbike.

106
00:07:00,940 --> 00:07:02,560
Nakagawa's a filthy-rich guy, after all.

107
00:07:02,560 --> 00:07:04,060
Reiko might not get it, though.

108
00:07:04,060 --> 00:07:05,820
That's just how human nature works.

109
00:07:05,820 --> 00:07:09,040
It's a Volvo.

110
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:10,040
Yo!

111
00:07:10,040 --> 00:07:12,560
for the meeting about
next week's survival game.

112
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:13,860
You've come at just the right time.

113
00:07:15,700 --> 00:07:16,700
If you were

114
00:07:16,700 --> 00:07:19,280
Momotaro and stormed this Ogre Island,

115
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:20,880
how would you go about slaying the demons?

116
00:07:21,580 --> 00:07:23,380
First, the Momotaro costume -

117
00:07:23,380 --> 00:07:23,840
change it.

118
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:24,700
Costume?

119
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:29,100
Flying a
"Best in Japan" flag and wearing flashy clothes like this,

120
00:07:29,100 --> 00:07:31,180
and on top of that,
attacking in broad daylight,

121
00:07:31,180 --> 00:07:34,640
you'd be spotted by the ogres right away and get beaten back.
That's the best you could hope for.

122
00:07:35,140 --> 00:07:36,640
If it were me, I'd attack at night.

123
00:07:37,020 --> 00:07:38,800
And I'd use a landing boat.

124
00:07:41,640 --> 00:07:44,600
Camouflage uniforms, of course.

125
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:47,440
As expected from an ex-Green Beret.

126
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:50,700
So we land from the back side of the island?

127
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:54,400
Naive. That's probably a minefield.

128
00:07:55,520 --> 00:07:57,400
I see. So how do we attack?

129
00:07:57,860 --> 00:08:01,260
We split into two groups
and land here and here.

130
00:08:01,760 --> 00:08:04,200
First, we use hawks instead of pheasants.

131
00:08:04,200 --> 00:08:07,820
We drop grenades nonstop to
disrupt the enemy's main base.

132
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:11,600
Then we release Dobermans,
the most dangerous kind of dog, all at once.

133
00:08:12,600 --> 00:08:15,780
Plus, we send gorillas into
the castle instead of monkeys.

134
00:08:17,060 --> 00:08:20,720
This three-minute attack will
leave the enemy's forces in shambles.

135
00:08:20,720 --> 00:08:22,840
Then we steal the treasure and run.

136
00:08:22,840 --> 00:08:25,760
This is Operation Island Storm!

137
00:08:25,760 --> 00:08:27,580
Brilliant, Volvo!

138
00:08:27,580 --> 00:08:30,060
There's no way a story like that exists.

139
00:08:30,060 --> 00:08:33,100
I think Cinderella
would be better after all.

140
00:08:33,100 --> 00:08:36,660
It can give dreams to both kids and the elderly,
after all.

141
00:08:36,660 --> 00:08:40,360
No way.
How is Cinderella giving anyone a dream?

142
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:43,740
That story only works because
Cinderella is beautiful.

143
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:49,060
She's beautiful, so out of dozens of women,
she caught the prince's eye.

144
00:08:53,940 --> 00:08:56,960
If she were ugly,
he wouldn't even give her a second look.

145
00:08:57,380 --> 00:08:58,820
You two should be angry.

146
00:08:58,820 --> 00:09:01,180
Why are you pointing at us?

147
00:09:01,180 --> 00:09:03,100
Are you saying we're ugly?

148
00:09:03,420 --> 00:09:05,120
Now, now, don't get so worked up.

149
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:07,580
Take Cinderella before her makeover,
for example.

150
00:09:07,580 --> 00:09:12,040
If he falls for her because he sees her working hard,
that's true love.

151
00:09:13,740 --> 00:09:18,840
If he picks the nasty sister and marries her,
saying he'll change her, that's true love too.

152
00:09:19,540 --> 00:09:24,560
In the end, Cinderella teaches that
only beautiful women can be happy!

153
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:27,700
How is that supposed to give anyone dreams?

154
00:09:29,840 --> 00:09:31,900
Besides, this whole story is full of lies.

155
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:36,560
The magic wears off at midnight,
and the dress and the carriage vanish,

156
00:09:36,560 --> 00:09:38,920
so why didn't the glass
slipper disappear too?

157
00:09:39,640 --> 00:09:42,080
Well, when you put it that way,
you're right.

158
00:09:43,740 --> 00:09:45,300
Even if the glass slipper was left behind,

159
00:09:45,300 --> 00:09:47,820
let's say its size is 24 centimeters.

160
00:09:47,820 --> 00:09:51,000
If you search the whole country, there
are bound to be women with feet that size,

161
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:52,260
at least fifty thousand of them.

162
00:09:52,260 --> 00:09:54,300
Saying only one Cinderella exists

163
00:09:54,300 --> 00:09:56,320
is completely unrealistic, isn't it?

164
00:09:56,700 --> 00:09:58,540
All you do is complain.

165
00:09:58,540 --> 00:10:01,800
Then what exactly are
we supposed to perform?

166
00:10:02,220 --> 00:10:02,580
Fine.

167
00:10:03,040 --> 00:10:04,980
Then I'll write a script

168
00:10:04,980 --> 00:10:09,220
that mixes Momotaro and Cinderella even further,
one that everyone will accept.

169
00:10:11,980 --> 00:10:13,720
This is definitely going to be a hit!

170
00:10:41,520 --> 00:10:46,480
What is Kankichi Ryotsu doing?

171
00:10:46,960 --> 00:10:55,640
You had us make the costumes, but if the script isn't finished, how can we perform?
Sorry, sorry, the script has finally been printed.

172
00:10:55,640 --> 00:10:58,020
Everyone, memorize your lines right away!

173
00:11:03,120 --> 00:11:07,180
Ladies and gentlemen, and dads and moms too.

174
00:11:07,660 --> 00:11:10,500
Next, the brave officers of
Katsushika Police Station present

175
00:11:10,500 --> 00:11:13,640
Broadway and Mazzao
Super present an old tale.

176
00:11:14,180 --> 00:11:16,560
It's Momotaro!
And now, the show begins, the show begins!

177
00:11:20,520 --> 00:11:23,220
Momotaro?
I've never heard that story, you know.

178
00:11:23,220 --> 00:11:23,980
Me neither.

179
00:11:23,980 --> 00:11:28,400
Once upon a time,
there lived an old man and an old woman.

180
00:11:30,820 --> 00:11:33,760
What's with this face?
It's a makeup disaster!

181
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,500
Why do I have to be the old woman,
seriously?

182
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:41,080
Hey, stop muttering over there.

183
00:11:41,080 --> 00:11:44,380
One day, the old man went to
the mountains to gather firewood,

184
00:11:45,060 --> 00:11:48,120
and the old woman went to
the river to do the laundry.

185
00:11:48,940 --> 00:11:51,780
As the old woman was
washing clothes in the river,

186
00:11:51,780 --> 00:11:55,360
a huge peach came thudding along,

187
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:57,560
bobbing along, right?

188
00:11:57,560 --> 00:12:01,260
and then it fell from the sky.

189
00:12:02,180 --> 00:12:03,700
That can't be right, can it?

190
00:12:05,880 --> 00:12:07,460
And four of them, no less.

191
00:12:07,460 --> 00:12:09,800
Isn't the story just like Momotaro?

192
00:12:09,800 --> 00:12:12,160
Wasn't it only one peach?

193
00:12:12,160 --> 00:12:14,680
It's a Peach-derella, so four is fine.

194
00:12:14,680 --> 00:12:19,140
The old woman immediately
took the four peaches home.

195
00:12:19,140 --> 00:12:20,680
Then the old man...

196
00:12:21,780 --> 00:12:24,600
What a fine peach!
Let's cut it open and eat it right away.

197
00:12:24,860 --> 00:12:26,420
Yaaah!

198
00:12:26,780 --> 00:12:29,860
Onyaa! Onyaa! Onyaa! Onyaa!

199
00:12:30,800 --> 00:12:32,220
Grandpa!

200
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:37,212
At the unbelievable event of
a baby being born from a peach,

201
00:12:37,262 --> 00:12:39,260
the old man with a heart condition...

202
00:12:40,680 --> 00:12:42,740
He suddenly passed away.

203
00:12:43,860 --> 00:12:45,860
Is that all my part is?

204
00:12:46,220 --> 00:12:50,080
Grandpa! Come back!

205
00:12:51,780 --> 00:12:54,140
The old woman was left holding a baby,
at a loss.

206
00:12:56,740 --> 00:12:59,800
But she couldn't just not raise the baby.

207
00:12:59,800 --> 00:13:02,580
The old woman took out her own breast

208
00:13:02,580 --> 00:13:04,560
and fed the baby.

209
00:13:06,400 --> 00:13:09,120
You can't possibly do
that in front of people!

210
00:13:11,400 --> 00:13:12,160
Excuse me.

211
00:13:12,160 --> 00:13:14,960
Because the old woman's
breasts were wrinkled and saggy,

212
00:13:14,960 --> 00:13:16,640
no milk came out.

213
00:13:16,640 --> 00:13:18,180
You still have the nerve to say that, you...

214
00:13:21,780 --> 00:13:24,560
Right, we have to give them names.

215
00:13:25,100 --> 00:13:28,280
First, since you're so handsome,
how about DiCaprio?

216
00:13:28,280 --> 00:13:30,020
That's a surname.

217
00:13:30,020 --> 00:13:33,360
In the end, no good names came to mind, so

218
00:13:33,360 --> 00:13:36,260
we took the easy route and just named
them after being born from a peach.

219
00:13:36,260 --> 00:13:40,400
We named them Momotaro,
Momojiro, Momoe, and Momoko.

220
00:13:40,900 --> 00:13:43,480
The children grew up in no time.

221
00:13:43,480 --> 00:13:46,620
Momotaro grew into a filial,
upstanding young man.

222
00:13:46,620 --> 00:13:48,100
Gokuraku, gokuraku.

223
00:13:48,100 --> 00:13:52,160
Momoko grew into a hardworking,
beautiful daughter, but

224
00:13:52,740 --> 00:13:56,120
Momojiro alone went astray
and joined a biker gang.

225
00:14:03,300 --> 00:14:05,140
I ran away from home.

226
00:14:05,140 --> 00:14:07,140
Big news! Big news!

227
00:14:08,100 --> 00:14:09,760
What's wrong, Momoko?

228
00:14:09,760 --> 00:14:15,220
Apparently, tonight at the castle, the prince is hosting
a swimming contest with nothing but women.

229
00:14:15,220 --> 00:14:16,620
What?! Say that again?

230
00:14:18,100 --> 00:14:20,680
If a gentleman takes a fancy to you,
you might become a bride.

231
00:14:22,980 --> 00:14:26,480
Prince,
wasn't this supposed to be a Japanese story?

232
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:30,800
In this age of internationalization,
the story isn't limited to Japan alone.

233
00:14:31,140 --> 00:14:32,960
We have to go global.

234
00:14:34,680 --> 00:14:35,760
This is...!

235
00:14:38,300 --> 00:14:41,460
Granny.
Why have you suddenly gotten younger?

236
00:14:41,780 --> 00:14:43,680
I'm the latest plastic surgery beauty,
you know.

237
00:14:43,680 --> 00:14:47,600
Wait, so why does only
Momoko have no swimsuit?

238
00:14:48,140 --> 00:14:52,000
We're poor,
so we could only afford two swimsuits.

239
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,760
That's because Granny goes and gets
plastic surgery and all that, right?

240
00:14:54,760 --> 00:14:59,560
Actually, the old woman was jealous
